        "findings": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is a faithful, additive mirror of the existing `namesakeCount` feature, and it lands each piece in the unit where its analog already lives:\n\n- **`Owner.java`** \u2014 new `householdSize` field + getter/setter + `toString` append, placed exactly alongside `namesakeCount`. Consistent.\n- **`ClinicService` / `ClinicServiceImpl`** \u2014 `countHouseholdMembers` sits next to `countNamesakes`/`countOwnersInCity` and uses the same `findAll().stream().filter(...).count()` idiom with the same null guard and `@Transactional(readOnly = true)`. It matches the established (if repetitive) house style rather than inventing a new one; introducing a generic helper here would actually diverge from the three sibling methods.\n- **`OwnerRestControllerV1`** \u2014 the derive-and-set happens in the same block as `bulkSignupWarning` and `namesakeCount`, which is the controller's established \"orchestrate derived fields before save\" pattern. The small `householdId == null ? 1 : \u2026 + 1` conditional is proportionate and well-commented.\n- **`OwnerMapper.formatMembershipTier`** \u2014 the GOLD branch is layered cleanly ahead of the existing SILVER/BRONZE logic, reading `householdSize` just as the method already reads `namesakeCount`; the Javadoc is updated to match.\n- **Schema files, OpenAPI description, and MapStruct `ignore` mappings** \u2014 all updated in lockstep with the `namesakeCount`/`householdId` precedents.\n\nNothing is duplicated beyond the deliberate existing idiom, no class or method is turning into a catch-all, and a maintainer familiar with the `namesakeCount` path would find this immediately natural."
